  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 108 AM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Showers and embedded storms will exist this morning \nthrough late afternoon, with the risk of a few severe storms.\n\nA cold front will be pushing through the region today and tonight as \nan area of low pressure tracks into the Lower Great Lakes. The main \nbatch of showers and embedded storms will be tied to an effective \npre-frontal moist axis. This boundary will track through during the \nmorning hours across the Piedmont and mid-late afternoon across the \nSandhills and Coastal Plain. The real cold front, bringing eventual \nlows tonight into the 40s to low 50s, will not arrive until \novernight tonight.\n\nIsolated showers and storms are present at the moment across the \nwestern and southern Piedmont. Coverage and intensity is expected to \nincrease during the mid to late morning hours and continue into the \nlate afternoon before exiting into eastern NC/VA by early this \nevening. \n\nThe severe weather threat today remains focused along and east of \nthe I-95 corridor, along and south of the pre-frontal moist axis. \nThe HRRR/RRFS suggest MLCAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg in this zone, within \na band of 35-40 kts of deep-layer shear. SPC, however, has expanded \nthe Marginal Risk to include not just the Coastal Plain and \nSandhills, but also the Triangle. This westward expansion of the \nthreat is likely tied to subtle variations in the position/timing of \nthe moist axis, shifting the instability axis as far west as Wake \nCounty. Most guidance depicts the instability axis straddling the US \nHwy 1 corridor by midday, thereby confining surface-based \ninstability to mainly areas along/east of US-1. Proximity soundings \nfrom the RAP/RRFS indicate this same pattern, with more elevated \nstorms along/west of US-1 and more surface-based convection east of \nthat line. The main threats are damaging winds, though we certainly \ncannot rule out a few brief short-track tornadoes from transient \nsupercells within line breaks, especially for areas along and east \nof I-95. The main timing for severe weather is between 10 AM and 4 \nPM, indicating a slightly faster trend in the latest 00z guidance.\n\nThere has been a notable trend in the QPF amounts and footprint in \nthe last few runs of the HRRR/REFS. Rainfall totals are presently \nexpected to range from a quarter inch to around an inch. However, \nlocalized totals between 1\" and 2.5\" will be possible, especially \nalong the south and eastward moving moist convergence axis. The \nHRRR/REFS are painting this footprint along the US-1 corridor, \nincluding portions of the Triangle. This would be some very \nbeneficial rainfall given the dry stretch we have had these past \nseveral weeks.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A dry stretch returns after today, though a pattern \nchange may be in the offing late next week.\n\nA dry stretch of weather is expected to return after today. Much of \nthe medium-range ensemble guidance shows northwest flow at mid-\nlevels into early next week. Cool high pressure building in this \nweekend could bring some patchy frost Sunday morning with middle to \nupper 30s expected. Thereafter, a warming trend looks to take hold \nwith highs again some 5-10 degrees above normal in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s come the early to middle part of next week.\n\nWhile the dry stretch looks to hold into at least the middle of next \nweek, ensemble guidance is highlighting a potential deep and potent \ntrough digging into the OH Valley to Mid-Atlantic region Thursday \ninto next weekend. There is presently large spread in the trough \nposition, thereby leading to low confidence in precipitation \nchances. However, some AI-models are showing a more favorable storm \ntrack for rainfall. Some solutions also indicate 850-mb temperatures \ndropping below freezing late next week, favoring below normal \ntemperatures.",
